        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Maintaining an intact nutrient supply in the food system flown on spacecraft is a critical issue for mission success and crew health.  Ground-based evidence indicates that some vitamins may be altered and fatty acids oxidized (and therefore rendered useless, or even dangerous) by long-term storage and by exposure to radiation, both of which will be issues for long-duration exploration missions in space.  
<P>The objective of this project was to identify the vitamins and amino acids that degrade in space by collecting data on the magnitude and time course of degradation of select nutritionals in space. This investigation studied the effects of radiation in space on complex organic molecules, such as vitamins and other compounds in food, in order to help researchers develop more stable and reliable nutritional countermeasures suitable for future long-duration missions to the moon and Mars. <P>
APPROACH:<br>
In this study, the stability of nutrients and supplements was investigated in samples exposed to spaceflight on the International Space Station (ISS). A total of 6 replicates of 5 different space food items (tortillas, almonds, salmon, broccoli au gratin, and dried apricots), a multivitamin, and a vitamin D supplement were packaged into 4 identical kits and were launched in 2006 on the space shuttle STS-122. After 13, 353, 596, and 880 days of spaceflight aboard the ISS, the kits were returned to Earth. Nine replicates of each food item and vitamin, from the same lots as those sent into space, remained in an environmental chamber on Earth to serve as controls at each time point. Vitamins, hexanal, oxygen radical absorbance capacity, and amino acids were measured in identical-lot food samples at each time point. Researchers want to identify vitamins and amino acids at risk for degradation in space food supply, as well as identify changes in fatty acids of foods flown on ISS and characterize degradation profiles of the unstable nutrients. In addition to the food and drug items, the kits were equipped with two temperature loggers and a dosimeter to measure radiation.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
There was no crew interaction with the contents of the kits, which were configured to prevent them from being inadvertently opened. One of the kits was immediately returned on STS-122 after 13 days, while the other three remained aboard ISS for 353, 596, and 880 days of spaceflight. Each kit was returned on subsequent Shuttle flights. The flight kits were analyzed in parallel with the eight ground control kits, identical to those flown. 
<P>
There was little to no effect of the flights on nutrient stability.  After 596 days of spaceflight, differences in intact vitamin concentrations due to duration of storage were observed for most foodstuffs, but generally, nutrients from flight samples did not degrade any faster than ground controls. This study provided the first set of spaceflight data for investigation of nutrient stability in the food system, and the results will help NASA design food systems for both ISS and space exploration missions.  The storage time could impact food logistics, where foods are often pre-positioned many months ahead of planned use.
        </div>

